WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
Splitting: random is not enough.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.421
A random split assumes every example is independent.

3
00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:07.173
Real data rarely is.

4
00:00:07.453 --> 00:00:10.781
Stratify when you have categories whose proportions matter.

5
00:00:11.061 --> 00:00:18.464
If 10 percent of documents are support tickets, a random split of a small dataset can put almost none of them in the test set.

6
00:00:18.744 --> 00:00:25.741
Stratified splitting allocates each category to each split in proportion, so every split has every kind of document.

7
00:00:26.021 --> 00:00:33.531
scikit-learn’s splitters (StratifiedKFold and relatives) do this for you; the lab implements it by hand so you can see it.

8
00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:35.731
Group when examples are related.

9
00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:50.496
An email and its reply, three messages from one support thread, ten tickets from one customer, five chunks of one long document: if one lands in training and another in test, the model has effectively seen part of the test answer.

10
00:00:50.776 --> 00:00:57.859
Group-aware splitting assigns whole groups to a single split (scikit-learn’s GroupKFold and StratifiedGroupKFold).

11
00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:00.891
Choose the group by asking what production looks like.

12
00:01:01.171 --> 00:01:08.211
If your model will see new customers, group by customer, so the test set measures performance on customers it never saw.

13
00:01:08.491 --> 00:01:11.115
Split by time when production is the future.

14
00:01:11.395 --> 00:01:20.717
If the model will run on next quarter’s documents, train on older data and test on the most recent, because vocabulary, products, and formats drift.

15
00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:24.773
A random split hides that drift; a time-based split measures it.

16
00:01:25.053 --> 00:01:33.864
These constraints compete in small datasets, which is why the lab fills each stratum with whole groups until it reaches its target, then moves on.

17
00:01:34.144 --> 00:01:38.453
Expect split sizes to be close to the requested fractions, not exact.
